.Dd November 7, 2026
.Dt DEFAULTS 1
.Os Darling
.Sh NAME
.Nm defaults
.Nd read and change user defaults
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl currentHost | Fl host Ar hostname
.Ar command
.Op Ar arguments ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
reads and changes the preferences that applications store with
CFPreferences and NSUserDefaults.
Each application keeps its settings in a domain, usually named after its
bundle identifier, which is a dictionary of keys and values.
Changes are written through CFPreferences, so applications see them the
next time they read their preferences.
.Pp
A
.Ar domain
is one of:
.Bl -tag -width "-app name"
.It Ar name
a domain name such as
.Li com.example.App .
.It Fl g , Fl globalDomain , Li NSGlobalDomain
the global domain, whose values every application sees.
.It Fl app Ar name
the domain of the application bundle
.Ar name Ns .app
in one of the standard Applications folders, or at the given path.
.It Ar path
a property list file; any argument that contains a slash is a path.
The
.Pa .plist
extension may be left out.
.El
.Sh COMMANDS
.Bl -tag -width "read-type domain key"
.It Cm read Op Ar domain Op Ar key
Print all domains, one domain, or the value of one key.
.It Cm read-type Ar domain key
Print the type of a key's value.
.It Cm write Ar domain key value
Set a key.
.It Cm write Ar domain dictionary
Replace the whole domain with a dictionary in property list text form.
.It Cm rename Ar domain old new
Rename a key.
.It Cm delete Ar domain Op Ar key
Remove a key, or the whole domain.
.Cm delete-all
does the same.
.It Cm import Ar domain path
Add the keys of a property list file to a domain.
Keys the file does not contain are kept.
A
.Ar path
of
.Li -
reads standard input.
.It Cm export Ar domain path
Save a domain as a binary property list, or with a
.Ar path
of
.Li - ,
print it as an XML property list.
.It Cm domains
List the domains of the current user.
.It Cm find Ar word
Print the keys whose name or string value contains
.Ar word ,
ignoring case, in every domain.
.It Cm help
Print a short usage text.
.El
.Sh VALUES
A value is either a property list in old-style text form, or a type flag
followed by its argument:
.Bl -tag -width "-bool[ean] value"
.It Fl string Ar string
.It Fl data Ar hex
hexadecimal digits
.It Fl int[eger] Ar number
.It Fl float Ar number
stored as a 32-bit floating point number
.It Fl bool[ean] Ar value
.Li yes ,
.Li no ,
.Li true
or
.Li false
in any case
.It Fl date Ar date
.Li "YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS +ZZZZ"
or an RFC 822 date; without a time zone the time is UTC
.It Fl array Ar value ...
.It Fl array-add Ar value ...
append to an existing array
.It Fl dict Ar key value ...
.It Fl dict-add Ar key value ...
add to an existing dictionary
.El
.Pp
A plain value that contains none of the characters
.Li "(){}<>[]\(dq"
is taken as a string as it is.
Arrays and dictionaries cannot be nested with type flags; use property list
text for their elements instead.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 when a domain or key does not exist or a value cannot be
parsed, and 255 for a usage error.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Dl defaults write com.example.App ShowToolbar -bool NO
.Dl defaults read com.example.App ShowToolbar
.Dl defaults write com.example.App Recent -array-add Document.txt
.Dl defaults write com.example.App Window '{ x = 10; y = 20; }'
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr plutil 1
